cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Swarmbly AI — the protocol, the specification, or the reference
  implementation — in academic work, please cite both the software (this
  file) and the whitepaper (see `preferred-citation` below).
type: software

title: "Swarmbly AI"
abstract: >-
  Peer-to-peer language-model inference today splits the MODEL: layers or
  tensors are spread across machines and activations cross the public internet
  on every generated token, against bandwidth and latency gaps of four to five
  orders of magnitude. Swarmbly is a protocol that distributes the PROBLEM
  instead. A client-side orchestrator decomposes a request into a dependency
  graph of semantic micro-tasks, each dispatched once and asynchronously to a
  volunteer node running a complete small language model (1–8B parameters);
  returned fragments are verified, selected and spliced locally, so the network
  is crossed once per fragment per session rather than once per layer per
  token. Three substantive contributions follow. First, the context budget S —
  the shared context carried by each dispatched fragment — is identified as a
  single scalar on which assembly coherence, fragment verifiability, privacy by
  decontextualization and required worker capability all depend, with opposing
  signs; viability reduces to whether some S satisfies all four thresholds at
  once, stated as a falsifiable proposition. Second, a coverage model for
  semantic assembly, in which the pre-generation plan acts as the reference
  sequence and packet loss rather than sample placement is the stochastic
  element, yields the design equation c >= ln(1/eps)/(1-p) and an operating
  range of 3–5 replicas per critical unit. Third, consensus by multiple
  alignment of replicas produced by nodes of deliberately different model
  families returns, with every answer, a per-unit agreement score and a map of
  low-confidence regions — a reliability signal that a provider running a
  single model has nothing to align, and therefore cannot produce. The release
  also specifies the wire protocol, the assembly and verification algorithms,
  and a reference harness that measures the coherence tax against a stated
  abandonment criterion. No claim is made of latency parity, unlimited context,
  cryptographic confidentiality or a demonstrated environmental benefit;
  limitations and negative results are stated in full. This release comprises
  whitepaper v1.4, protocol specification v0.2 (revision 2), and the V0 reference
  implementation.

