references:
  - type: conference-paper
    title: "Petals: Collaborative Inference and Fine-tuning of Large Models"
    abstract: >-
      Referenced as the closest prior system in the distributed-inference
      space. Petals shards a single large model across participants; Swarmbly
      is contrasted against it because it shards the problem instead. The
      authoritative bibliography, with key figures and the reasoning behind
      each entry, is REFERENCES.md in this project.
    authors:
      - given-names: "Alexander"
        family-names: "Borzunov"
      - given-names: "Dmitry"
        family-names: "Baranchuk"
      - given-names: "Tim"
        family-names: "Dettmers"
      - given-names: "Max"
        family-names: "Ryabinin"
      - given-names: "Younes"
        family-names: "Belkada"
      - given-names: "Artem"
        family-names: "Chumachenko"
      - given-names: "Pavel"
        family-names: "Samygin"
      - given-names: "Colin"
        family-names: "Raffel"
    year: 2023
    collection-title: "Proceedings of the 61st Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 3: System Demonstrations)"
    url: "https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-demo.54/"
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "arXiv:2209.01188"
        description: "arXiv preprint identifier"
    notes: >-
      Cited as prior art, not as a dependency. The full-length follow-up
      (arXiv:2312.08361) carries the measurements this project relies on most:
      2.29 steps/s at 1 Gbit/s and under 5 ms RTT falling to 1.57 steps/s at
      100 Mbit/s and 100 ms RTT, which is roughly 31 percent of throughput lost
      to the network alone.

preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: >-
    Swarmbly: Decentralized AI Inference by Fragmenting the Problem, Not the
    Model
  abstract: >-
    Whitepaper v1.4 specifying the Swarmbly protocol: semantic decomposition of
    a request into micro-tasks, asynchronous dispatch to volunteer nodes each
    running a complete small language model, and local reassembly by a
    client-side orchestrator. Its three substantive contributions are the
    context budget S as the single scalar governing coherence, verifiability,
    privacy and required worker capability; a coverage model for semantic
    assembly giving the design equation c >= ln(1/eps)/(1-p); and consensus by
    multiple alignment of cross-family replicas, which returns a per-unit
    agreement score and a map of low-confidence regions that centralized
    inference cannot produce. Limitations and negative results are stated in
    full.
  authors:
    - given-names: "Sebastián"
      family-names: "Espinoza"
      affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
      email: "sebas_saeu@hotmail.com"
  year: 2026
  month: 8
  # Paper DOI: the Zenodo preprint record, which carries the whitepaper's full
  # academic title. An arXiv DOI (10.48550/arXiv.YYMM.NNNNN) will be added
  # alongside it if and when the submission clears endorsement; it replaces
  # nothing, because the Zenodo record is already the citable one.
  doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21957088"
  url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21957088"
  # journal: not applicable while it is a preprint
  notes: >-
    Preprint, deposited on Zenodo 2026-08-14. Intended additional venue: arXiv,
    primary category cs.DC — deferred, because a first-time submitter to that
    archive requires endorsement and none had been obtained at time of
    publication. The prior-art position does not depend on arXiv: the Zenodo
    DOIs and the public repository are independent dated records.
